    "description": "Iran’s stepped settlements arrange buildings and pedestrian spaces along mountain slopes. Masouleh and Hawraman/Uramanat illustrate different regional expressions of this architecture, where rooftops, paths, cultivated terraces, and working households form interconnected landscapes.",
    "tags": ["Vernacular architecture", "Mountain settlements", "Hawraman", "Masouleh", "Cultural landscapes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:31:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:31:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Stepped Villages in Iran: Architecture and Living Landscapes",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s stepped settlements through Masouleh and Hawraman, their shared rooftops, mountain livelihoods, and conservation challenges."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-slope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading a settlement through its slope",
        "content": "A stepped settlement is best understood in section: buildings occupy successive levels of a hillside, and circulation follows the differences in height. At Masouleh, also spelled Masuleh, roofs and courtyards form part of the pedestrian network. In Hawraman/Uramanat, UNESCO identifies public rooftops and terraced village layouts as characteristic features of the protected landscape. Similar spatial solutions do not make these places a single cultural tradition; their regional settings and livelihoods deserve separate attention. [s1][s2]\n\nFor visitors, this architecture changes what it means to look at a building. A roof can also participate in the space of the settlement around it. The useful question is therefore not simply how houses are stacked, but how residents move, work, and share space across the slope. This is an architectural reading of the documented layouts, rather than a claim that every rooftop is publicly accessible. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "masouleh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Masouleh: interconnected roofs and paths",
        "content": "Masouleh lies in the Alborz Mountains near the Caspian Sea. ICESCO’s heritage description explains how its buildings follow the mountain slope, with interconnected courtyards and roofs serving pedestrian movement. Stairs and narrow passages are integral to this arrangement. The same account places the town in a landscape of substantial rainfall, forest, and fog. [s2]\n\nThese features offer a useful way to examine the settlement without treating it as a collection of picturesque façades. Follow the relationship between a doorway, the path beside it, and the roof below. Notice how a continuous pedestrian route can pass across several building levels. The visual impression of a compact hillside town emerges from these connections, rather than from isolated houses. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hawraman",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hawraman: buildings within a working landscape",
        "content": "Hawraman/Uramanat, in the Zagros Mountains of Kurdistan and Kermanshah provinces, connects stepped settlements with terraced cultivation and seasonal livestock movement. UNESCO inscribed the cultural landscape in 2021. Its significance includes the relationships between villages, gardens, migration routes, and seasonal dwellings. [s1]\n\nThe nomination management plan distinguishes permanent village houses from havars, upland seasonal settlements associated with grazing. It describes movement between these settings as responsive to temperature, pasture, and water. Village life and seasonal mobility consequently belong to the same account of land use. A visitor who sees only the tiered houses misses this wider geography. [s3]\n\nThe plan also explains the importance of orchard terraces where flat cultivable ground is scarce. Agricultural terraces and stepped houses are different structures, but together they reveal how settlement and production accommodate mountain terrain. Their relationship is more informative than a photograph of the skyline alone. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Conserving places that remain inhabited",
        "content": "UNESCO’s assessment of Hawraman notes that modern materials and infrastructure can affect historic character, while continued farming helps sustain the landscape. Conservation therefore involves everyday uses as well as building fabric. [s1]\n\nAn appropriate editorial approach to visiting follows from that distinction: appreciate routes and roofscapes while respecting household boundaries, and ask locally before entering spaces whose access is unclear. The lasting interest of stepped settlements lies in how people inhabit difficult terrain, maintain connections, and adapt their surroundings over time.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
