    "description": "Iran’s rural handicrafts connect materials, agricultural work, household knowledge, and specialist skills. Carpet weaving in Fars, silk production, and wooden boatbuilding on the Persian Gulf show why understanding a craft requires attention to its production process and the people who transmit it.",
    "tags": ["Handicrafts", "Carpet weaving", "Sericulture", "Fars", "Living heritage", "Craft knowledge"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:31:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:31:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Rural Handicrafts in Iran: Materials, Makers and Knowledge",
      "description": "Understand Iranian rural crafts through Fars carpet weaving, silk production, and the connected skills of Persian Gulf boatbuilding."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "craft-as-knowledge",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than a finished object",
        "content": "A useful introduction to rural handicrafts begins before the finished product. Fars carpet weaving connects wool preparation, loom construction, dyeing, design, and weaving. Silk production begins with growing feed for silkworms. Persian Gulf wooden boatbuilding belongs to a larger body of maritime knowledge. These examples show why a craft is also a relationship between materials, work, and learning. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThey also establish the limits of a single national label. A carpet, a silk textile, and a working vessel involve different environments and purposes. This page follows selected documented processes rather than proposing one technique or appearance as representative of all Iranian villages. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fars-weaving",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Fars carpet weaving",
        "content": "UNESCO’s account of traditional carpet weaving in Fars, southwestern Iran, describes a sequence beginning with sheep shearing and the preparation of a horizontal loom. Wool is spun into yarn, and dyes include plant materials such as madder, indigo, walnut skin, and pomegranate skin. Its description emphasizes women’s work in choosing colors, developing designs, and weaving, alongside men’s roles in shearing and loom construction. [s1]\n\nThe account also describes weaving without a drawn design template and learning through oral instruction and demonstration within families. This is a documented account of the inscribed tradition, not a survey proving that every contemporary Fars workshop follows identical practices. For a visitor, asking how a maker learned, which materials were used, and how a pattern developed can reveal more than identifying a motif alone. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "silk",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Silk links farming with textile work",
        "content": "Sericulture means raising silkworms for silk. UNESCO’s multinational inscription, which includes Iran, describes farmers tending silkworms and growing mulberry trees for their leaves. Fibers are reeled from cocoons and made into threads, which are cleaned, dyed, and used in woven products. The documented outputs include fabrics, carpets, rugs, and curtains. [s2]\n\nThis sequence makes an important distinction between producing a fiber and weaving a finished textile. Someone demonstrating a loom may explain only one stage in a much longer chain. An informative encounter can follow that chain backward: where the thread came from, who prepared it, and which stages the workshop performs. The inscription supplies a shared process description; it does not establish that every Iranian silk-producing locality uses the same equipment or division of labor. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "working-crafts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Crafts can be large working technologies",
        "content": "Handicraft also encompasses objects far larger than souvenirs. UNESCO’s Lenj entry describes traditionally hand-built wooden vessels used along the northern Persian Gulf for activities including trade and fishing. Boat construction belongs to a knowledge system that also includes navigation, weather interpretation, terminology, and work songs. [s3]\n\nThese examples suggest a practical way to approach a demonstration: ask which part of the process is being shown, distinguish observation from participation, and agree on any purchase or teaching arrangement with the maker. The aim is to understand skilled work in its own setting, including the decisions and learning that a finished object cannot display.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
